WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.540
Irish policies promoted the single lumper potato to feed a growing population, but one disease

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.040
exposed the risk of genetic uniformity.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.400
This is unintended consequences, episode 131.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:19.160
In the spring of 1845, Irish smallholders watched their fields turn black almost overnight

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.820
as a previously unknown mold swept across the island.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:29.220
The same variety that had allowed families to survive on tiny rented plots now offered no

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:35.020
variation that might have resisted the pathogen. This was designed to increase food output on

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:41.340
limited land. Instead, it removed the genetic diversity that had once buffered earlier crop failures.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.460
British administrators and improving landlords in the early 19th century saw the lumper potato

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:54.140
as a practical answer to rapid population growth on subdivided holdings. The variety produced

00:00:54.140 --> 00:01:00.640
reliable calories, even on poor soil, and required little capital, which matched the economic realities

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.700
of tenant farmers who had few other options. At the time, raising output per acre appeared the

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:13.180
most direct way to reduce rural poverty and the pressure on poor relief systems. Officials therefore

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:24.120
encouraged or required tenants to devote more ground to the high-yielding lumper, rather than to

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:29.920
assumption that greater food supply would stabilize society without requiring larger structural

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.300
changes in land tenure.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.000
One can see the arithmetic they faced.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.180
A single acre planted in Lumpur could furnish the bulk of a household's annual energy

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.620
needs where wheat or oats would have demanded far more acreage and manure, leaving little

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.680
margin once rent was paid in kind.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:53.020
Skeptics who pointed to older potato strains or grain rotations were answered with the immediate

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.340
evidence that Lumpur let families remain on holdings that would otherwise have become

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:03.260
unviable under existing lease terms. By the 1830s, the Lumpur had become the dominant

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:09.520
crop on small farms across much of Lanster, Munster, and Conoct. Agricultural societies

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.000
and estate managers distributed seed and praised the variety's productivity in printed reports

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.680
and at local shows. Early results looked promising. Population continued to rise and seasonal

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.320
hunger became less frequent in many districts. A few observers noted that dependence on one

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:32.780
variety reduced dietary diversity, yet these cautions were treated as secondary to the

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:38.420
immediate gain in calories. Within two decades, the island's potato acreage was overwhelmingly

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:44.420
lumper, setting the stage for a single point failure. The shift was incremental rather than

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:49.640
decreed. Each season's choice reinforced the next because the lumpers higher tuber count

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:54.660
per plant, translated directly into more barrels that could be stored or used to meet the

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:00.580
landlord's share. Over time, the older, more fibrous varieties that had once occupied the corners

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:06.420
of fields or served as insurance against wet summers simply disappeared from most small plots,

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:12.200
not through prohibition but through the quiet pressure of comparative yield. When late blight

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:18.780
reached Ireland in 1845, the genetically uniform lumper offered no resistant individuals that might

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:24.100
have survived and produced seed for the next season. The pathogens spread through the damp summer

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:31.680
and destroyed stored tubers as well as standing crops, so that by 1846 and 1847 almost no

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.500
sound seed remained for replanting. Because smallholders had come to rely on the potato for both

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:48.760
food and the rent that was paid in kind, the loss triggered immediate destitution rather than a

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.820
could not reach everyone in time, and the absence of alternative local crops magnified

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:58.700
the scale of hunger. Roughly one million people died, and another million emigrated.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:04.660
The same uniformity that had maximized yield in good years eliminated the natural buffer

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:10.560
that mixed plantings had previously supplied against disease shocks. Secondary effects included

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:16.340
the collapse of rural credit networks, the acceleration of land consolidation, and long-term

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.120
demographic decline that persisted for generations.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.480
One further consequence followed from the first.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:28.020
Once the lumper failed, the very small size of holdings that the potato had made viable

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:33.520
now left families with no fallback crop and no accumulated surplus to purchase seed

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:34.580
of any other kind.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.760
The absence of genetic variation meant the failure was total rather than partial.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:45.160
Removing the scattered survivors that, in a mixed planting, might have supplied the next

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:51.000
years tubers even after a poor season. After the immediate crisis, farmers gradually

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.300
reintroduced other potato varieties and diversified into oats, turnips, and livestock.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.500
Agricultural research later emphasized breeding for resistance rather than maximum yield alone.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:07.860
No formal policy reversal occurred because the original encouragement had been informal

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:13.080
and market-driven, yet the lived experience shifted planting practices across the island.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:19.020
Instead, it left a lasting preference for varietal diversity in Irish agriculture that

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:19.900
continues today.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:27.820
The practical memory of 1845 and 1846 translated into a reluctance on holdings of every size

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:33.980
to stake an entire season on any single strain, a habit that persisted even after new potato

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.340
types became available.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.160
Yield-focused incentives can crowd out the redundancy that complex biological systems

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:47.320
rely on when conditions change. When a single metric calories per acre guides adoption across

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:53.660
an entire region, the resulting uniformity creates hidden systemic exposure. Decision makers

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:59.340
therefore benefit from asking what diversity is being sacrificed in pursuit of any one performance

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:05.940
measure. How might current efforts to standardize seed, code or supply chains be creating similar

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:12.100
single points of failure. That's unintended consequences for today. If this episode gave you

